msgid "Sapphire, Ruby, Emerald, Amethyst, Amber, Aquamarine, Diamond: dark themes with colors of their own."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:118
msgid "Accent Color:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:118
msgid "The color of the selected row in the program's own lists (User Guide, Saved Combos, the Settings drop-downs) and of the button that waits for a key."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:118
msgid "Default: follows the theme: blue in the dark theme (the color of the theme in the colored themes) and the Windows accent color in the light one."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:119
msgid "Colorful Icons"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:119
msgid "Draw the icons of the menus, the toolbar and the favorite star in color, one color for each kind of action. Off draws them black and white, in the color of the text."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:120
msgid "High Contrast Mode"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:120
msgid "While Windows is in a high-contrast mode, use the Light theme and the Classic style (the program's own colors are left off, so the windows use the colors Windows chose). Style, Theme and Accent Color are grayed while this is on (untick it to change them); your own choice comes back when high contrast is turned off."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:121
msgid "Interface Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:121
msgid "Normal or Large menus, dialogs and toolbar. Changing it restarts the program (it asks first, and your game is opened again)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:122
msgid "Toolbar Icon Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:122
msgid "How big the buttons of the toolbar are: Small, Normal (the default) or Large (also in View > Toolbars > Toolbar Size)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:124
msgid "Covers"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:125
msgid "Cover Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:125
msgid "How big the covers are in the grid view, from Tiny to Extra Large (also in View > View Mode > Grid Size). Only with the Grid View chosen. Ctrl + mouse wheel over the grid sets any size in between."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:126
msgid "Cover Frame:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:126
msgid "The shape of the covers in the grid view: Square, Box (portrait, like a game case) or Wide."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:127
msgid "Rounded Cover Corners"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:127
msgid "Round the corners of the covers in the grid view."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:128
msgid "Show Name Under Covers"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:128
msgid "Show the game's name under each cover in the grid view; switch it off for only the pictures."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:129
msgid "Frame Around Cover and Name"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:129
msgid "Draw a frame around each cover and its name in the grid view, so every game looks like a card."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:130
msgid "Region Flags"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:130
msgid "A small flag for each region of a game (taken from tags in the file name such as (USA) or (Japan, Europe)): instead of the region text in the Region column of the list (a game without a region tag gets a gray-blue flag with a question mark), and in the bottom left corner of each cover in the grid view. Europe's flag stands for the countries of Europe and the World flag for Asia: for those the name is written after the flag. Switch it off to see the region text again."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:131
msgid "Console Badge on Covers"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:131
msgid "Draw a small tag (MD, MCD, 32X, SMS, GG, SG, PICO) in the corner of each cover in the grid view, to tell the consoles apart at a glance."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:132
msgid "Keep a Cover Cache"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:132
msgid "Save each finished cover in one file (Images\\Covers\\thumbs.dat), so the next time the program starts the covers appear almost at once instead of being read and scaled again. A cover that changes, or a new size or frame, is made again by itself. The game list keeps what it found out about each file (Covers\\library.dat) too, so the list loads faster."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:133
msgid "Clear Cover Cache"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:133
msgid "Delete the saved covers and the saved game list information; they are made again as you browse."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:136
msgid "Program Behavior"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:137
msgid "Pause When Inactive"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:137
msgid "Pause the game while another window has focus."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:138
msgid "Mute When Inactive"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:138
msgid "Silence the game while another window has focus (the game keeps running; see Pause When Inactive to stop it)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:139
msgid "Keep Only One Instance"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:139
msgid "Starting the program again (or opening a game with it) brings the copy that is already running to the front, and opens the game there, instead of starting a second copy. Two copies share the same settings and save files and would overwrite each other. Takes effect the next time the program starts. Launches from a frontend (--exit-on-close) are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:140
msgid "Always on Top"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:140
msgid "Keep the window above every other window (also in the View menu)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:141
msgid "Keep the PC Awake While Playing"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:141
msgid "Stop Windows from sleeping, dimming the screen or starting the screen saver while a game is running."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:142
msgid "Confirm Exit While Playing"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:142
msgid "Ask \"Exit?\" when you close the program while a game is running."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:145
msgid "Data Folders..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:145
msgid "Choose where each folder beside the program is: BIOS, Saves, States, Cheats, Covers, Recordings, Screenshots, Languages, Shaders, Bezels, Profiles and Logs. Files already in the old place are not moved."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:146
msgid "ROM Directories..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:146
msgid "Choose the directories the game list is made of: add more or remove some, and exclude some from the scan."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:154
msgid "Window and Screen"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:155
msgid "Window Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:155
msgid "Size of the game window, 1x to 10x the console's picture."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:156
msgid "Lock Window Shape When Resizing"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:156
msgid "When you drag an edge or a corner of the game window, keep it the shape of the picture (no black bars). Switch it off to drag the window to any shape; the picture inside is still drawn in its right shape (Aspect Ratio), with bars where the window is another shape."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:157
msgid "Fullscreen Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:157
msgid "How the game goes fullscreen."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:157
msgid "Borderless Window: fills the screen without changing it."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:157
msgid "Exclusive Fullscreen: switches the screen to the Fullscreen Resolution while the game is fullscreen."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:158
msgid "Fullscreen Resolution:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:158
msgid "The screen size used in fullscreen when Fullscreen Mode is Exclusive Fullscreen: the screen is switched to it while the game is fullscreen and put back after. Desktop Resolution changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:159
msgid "Start ROM in Fullscreen"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:159
msgid "Start every game in fullscreen. Esc or Alt+Enter toggles it."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:161
msgid "Scaling and Shape"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:162
msgid "Render Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:162
msgid "Pixel-art scalers and CRT looks (Scale2x, xBR, HQ2x, xBRZ, Scanlines, CRT...) applied before the picture is shown, at a fixed multiple (2x to 6x). For smooth resizing to the window use Scaling Filter below (Nearest, Bilinear, Sharp Bilinear, Bicubic). They run on the processor: xBRZ 5x and 6x and CRT Geom are very heavy and can make a game run below full speed (watch the frame rate); 2x to 4x are much lighter, and a shader below is lighter still."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Scaling Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "How the picture is resized to the window, after the Render Filter."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Nearest: sharp pixels, but uneven ones at a size that is not a whole multiple (use Integer Scaling for even ones)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Sharp Nearest: the picture is first enlarged by a whole multiple with sharp pixels, then smoothed only for the rest of the way: crisp, even pixels without the borders of Integer Scaling. Looks close to Nearest."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Bilinear: soft edges everywhere."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Sharp Bilinear: like Bilinear with much less blur: the middle of each pixel stays sharp and only the seam between two pixels is blended, over about half a source pixel (a pixel shader with Direct3D 9 and 11, the processor with the classic renderer)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Bicubic: smoother than Bilinear with a little more edge contrast, Catmull-Rom (Direct3D 9 and 11; grayed with the classic renderer)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:163
msgid "Grayed while a shader runs (Direct3D 11): the shader scales the picture itself, and your choice comes back when it is off."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:164
msgid "Integer Scaling"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:164
msgid "Enlarge the picture by whole multiples only (1x, 2x, 3x ...), so every pixel is the same size. It is centered, with borders, when the window is between two sizes."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "Aspect Ratio:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "The shape of the picture's pixels."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "Console's Own (default): the Mega Drive's own pixels, 8:7 wide in the 256-pixel mode and 32:35 in the 320-pixel one; other consoles use square pixels."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "1:1: square pixels."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "4:3: as on a CRT television."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:165
msgid "16:9: stretches the picture to fill a widescreen window."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:166
msgid "Rotation:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:166
msgid "Turn the picture by a quarter, half or three quarters of a turn, for games and demos made to be played with the screen on its side. Only what is on the screen is turned: screenshots, recordings and light-gun aiming are not."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:167
msgid "Borders:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:167
msgid "Hide or show the overscan borders (top/bottom, left/right). This covers every console; the Master System side borders and the Game Gear screen have their own options in Settings > Options."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:168
msgid "Interlaced Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:168
msgid "For games that use the 448-line interlaced mode. Not used while Deinterlacing (Visual Effects) is on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:168
msgid "Single Field: shows the same half of the picture every frame, so it stays steady."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:168
msgid "Double Field: shows both fields at once; where something moves, the older field's lines are rebuilt from the new ones (the new lines stay exactly as the game drew them), so there are hardly any comb teeth and a moving object stays sharp (a pattern that repeats and scrolls, such as blades of grass, is found too); a still picture keeps both fields."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:170
msgid "Bezel and Border"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:171
msgid "Bezel Image..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:171
msgid "Draw an image (a PNG with a see-through middle: a TV, an arcade cabinet) over the picture as a frame. Shown in fullscreen only."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:172
msgid "Remove Bezel"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:172
msgid "Go back to plain bars around the picture."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:173
msgid "Border Color:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:173
msgid "The color of the bars around the picture when the window or screen is a different shape."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:176
msgid "Filters and Effects"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:177
msgid "NTSC Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:177
msgid "Composite, S-Video or RGB television look. For a 32X game it is the program's own version of the look (the picture keeps its size), not the Genesis Plus GX core's."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:178
msgid "Color Correction:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:178
msgid "The colors of a handheld LCD screen instead of the raw ones, for every console: paler and a little dull, the colors bleeding into each other (as on the Game Gear screen)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:178
msgid "Light: half way between the raw colors and the LCD."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:178
msgid "Full: the LCD colors."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:178
msgid "Hue, Saturation and the other Picture Adjustments still apply on top."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:179
msgid "LCD Ghosting:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:179
msgid "Bright pixels fade slowly, like a handheld screen."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:180
msgid "Scanlines:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:180
msgid "Dark lines between rows: Off, 25, 50, 75 or 100%."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:181
msgid "For games that use the 448-line interlaced mode (Sonic the Hedgehog 2 in 2P Vs., Ys III, Air Strike Patrol ...): the graphics processor draws both fields of the picture from the same moment in every frame, so every frame is a whole picture and nothing that moves is combed. While it is on, Interlaced Mode is not used."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:182
msgid "Dither Smoothing"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:182
msgid "Smooths the checkerboard patterns many Genesis games use to fake extra colors."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:183
msgid "Flicker Blending"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:183
msgid "Some games fake transparency by showing an object every other frame. This blends the two frames so it looks solid."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:184
msgid "Brighten"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:184
msgid "A gentle brightness lift."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:185
msgid "Picture Adjustments..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:185
msgid "Open the window with the Brightness, Contrast, Saturation, Gamma and Hue sliders (also in the Video menu)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:187
msgid "Speed and Smoothness"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:188
msgid "Renderer:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:188
msgid "GDI draws with the CPU; Direct3D 9 and 11 use the graphics card (each falls back to GDI if unavailable). Shaders need Direct3D 11."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:189
msgid "VSync"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:189
msgid "Wait for the screen's refresh after each frame so motion is even. Skipped for a frame whenever the sound buffer runs low, so it never causes crackle. Not used (grayed out) while Variable Refresh Rate is on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:190
msgid "Variable Refresh Rate"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:190
msgid "For a screen with variable refresh (G-Sync, FreeSync): each frame is shown the moment it is ready, with no tearing and no frame shown twice or skipped. Direct3D 11 only; needs Windows 10 and a driver that supports it; it takes the place of VSync while it is on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:191
msgid "Limit Redraws:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:191
msgid "Draw the picture at most this many times a second while the game still runs at full speed. Helps a weak PC or a very fast monitor; Off draws every frame. Unlike Frameskip, which skips drawing only when the PC falls behind, this is a fixed cap on how often the picture is drawn."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:192
msgid "Frameskip:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:192
msgid "Skip drawing some frames on a slow PC so the game keeps up: Off, Auto, or Manual by audio-buffer level. Unlike Limit Redraws (a fixed cap), it skips drawing only when the PC falls behind, so the picture is choppier only then. Not used during netplay or recording."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:195
msgid "Shader:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:195
msgid "Run a librashader (.slangp) shader chain on top of the picture. Direct3D 11 only."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:196
msgid "Shader Parameters..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:196
msgid "Adjust the loaded shader's own parameters (sharpness, curvature ...) while it runs, without editing its preset file. Grayed until a shader that has settings is loaded."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:197
msgid "Skip Render Filter with Shaders"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:197
msgid "While a shader runs, do not apply the Render Filter first. The shader then gets the game's own picture instead of one the filter already enlarged. Shaders that scale by whole multiples or place a bezel work out their sizes from the picture they receive, so one that looks too small or wrong with a Render Filter on may look right with this ticked. Has no effect without a shader or a Render Filter."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:204
msgid "Mute All Sound"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:204
msgid "Silence the sound; untick to hear it again."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:205
msgid "Silence the FM chip (the main music) and keep the rest."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:206
msgid "Silence the PSG chip (the square-wave sound) and keep the rest."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:207
msgid "Silence the DAC, the sample channel of the Genesis FM chip (drums, voices and sound effects played as recorded sound), and keep the rest. The level is in Audio > Levels and Latency (DAC Volume)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:208
msgid "Silence the PWM sound of a 32X game (most of its music and effects) and keep the rest. Grayed out only while a game of another system is running. Its level is the PWM Preamp slider on the Levels and Latency window."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:210
msgid "Tone"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:211
msgid "Headphone Crossfeed:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:211
msgid "For headphones: lets a little of each ear's sound, with the highs taken off, into the other ear, which softens the hard left and right of the Mega Drive's stereo. Off, Light, Medium or Strong."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:212
msgid "Stereo Width:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:212
msgid "Narrow pulls the left and right sound toward the middle; wide spreads them apart."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:213
msgid "Bass Boost:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:213
msgid "Lift the low notes so the music is fuller on laptop speakers and small headphones."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:214
msgid "A soft high-frequency roll-off. Only one of Low-Pass Filter and 3-Band EQ can be on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:215
msgid "A three-band equalizer: raise or lower the low, middle and high sounds. Set the gains and the two crossover frequencies in Levels and Latency. Only one of Low-Pass Filter and 3-Band EQ can be on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:216
msgid "Mono Output"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:216
msgid "Mix the sound to mono instead of stereo."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:217
msgid "Overdrive"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:217
msgid "Push the sound harder into a soft limiter: louder, fuller and a little gritty, like a console turned up. Off keeps the clean sound."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:218
msgid "Even Out Loudness"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:218
msgid "Lower very loud games and lift quiet ones so they sit at a similar level, without clipping."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:219
msgid "Levels and Latency..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:219
msgid "Master volume, FM and PSG levels, CD audio, how many frames of sound are buffered, the audio filter (Off, Low-Pass or 3-Band EQ) and the Sega CD PCM volume."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:222
msgid "Mega Drive Sound Chip"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:223
msgid "FM Chip:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:223
msgid "Which Genesis FM sound chip is emulated."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:223
msgid "YM2612, YM3438: the Nuked versions (default YM2612 Nuked) or MAME."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:223
msgid "A 32X game (PicoDrive) has only the two chip types, so the YM3438 kinds play as a YM3438 and the others as a YM2612."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:224
msgid "High-Quality FM Synthesis"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:224
msgid "Band-limited FM synthesis: cleaner high notes, for a little more CPU. For a 32X game it switches PicoDrive's FM filtering."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:226
msgid "Master System Sound Chip"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "FM Unit:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "Whether the Master System's FM sound unit is emulated."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "Auto: turns it on when the game uses it."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "Off: never."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "On: always."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:227
msgid "Changing it restarts the sound chip, so some instruments may stay quiet until the game sends them again."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:228
msgid "Which chip emulates the Master System's FM sound unit."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:230
msgid "Sound Quality"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:231
msgid "High-Quality PSG Resampling"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:231
msgid "Cleaner sound from the PSG (the console's square-wave chip)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:232
msgid "Sample Rate:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:232
msgid "Audio sample rate: 22050 Hz (lighter, duller, for a slow PC), 44100 or 48000 Hz."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:234
msgid "Output"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "Audio Backend:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "How the sound reaches your speakers."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "Windows Classic: the long-standing way."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "XAudio2, WASAPI: newer Windows sound systems that can have lower delay and cope better when the PC is busy."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "Exclusive: gives the game the sound device for itself: the lowest delay, but no other program can play sound on that device while the game runs."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:235
msgid "If one does not work on your PC, the game falls back (Exclusive to Shared, then to Windows Classic). Sound Device applies to all of them."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:236
msgid "Sound Device:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:236
msgid "Which speakers or headphones the game plays through. Windows Default follows the Windows setting."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:242
msgid "Messages"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:243
msgid "Show Messages"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:243
msgid "Show short messages over the picture, such as \"Saved to slot 3\" or the screenshot name. Off hides them all (the status bar still shows them)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:244
msgid "Messages for Changed Options"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:244
msgid "When you change an option, show its name and new value (\"Color Correction: Light\"). Needs Show Messages."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:245
msgid "Message Position:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:245
msgid "Which corner of the picture the messages appear in."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:246
msgid "Messages Shown:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:246
msgid "How many of the latest messages stay on screen together: the newest nearest the corner, older ones stacked beside it."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:247
msgid "Message Time:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:247
msgid "How long a message stays on screen."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:250
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:251
msgid "Font Color:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:251
msgid "The color of messages, the frame rate and the pause menu title. The outline turns light for dark colors, so the text stays readable on any picture."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:252
msgid "Font Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:252
msgid "The size of the text of messages, the frame rate and the pause menu."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:254
msgid "Frame Rate"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:255
msgid "Show Frame Rate"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:255
msgid "Show frames per second on screen."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:256
msgid "Frame Rate Position:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:256
msgid "Which corner of the picture the frame rate appears in."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:262
msgid "Controllers"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:263
msgid "Controls..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:263
msgid "Set keyboard keys and gamepad buttons for Player 1 and Player 2, the hotkeys and the stick deadzone."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:264
msgid "Hotkeys..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:264
msgid "Change the keyboard shortcuts: Save State, Pause, Fullscreen and the rest."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Stick Directions:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "How the left stick makes the D-pad's diagonals."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Classic: compares each direction with the deadzone on its own, so a small push off a straight line already adds a second direction."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "The other choices use the angle of the push (the deadzone is only how far it has to go):"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Wide Straight Zones: keep running straight on a drifting stick (Sonic does not roll)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Even Eight Directions: gives every direction the same share."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Wide Diagonal Zones: suit games that need diagonals."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:265
msgid "Each entry of the open list shows its own tip beside the list."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:266
msgid "Pause When the Pad Disconnects"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:266
msgid "Pause the game with a message when Player 1's gamepad is unplugged or its battery runs out, instead of running on without input."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:267
msgid "Enable Background Input"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:267
msgid "Keep reading the keyboard pad keys and gamepads while another window has focus."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:270
msgid "Mouse Speed:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:270
msgid "How fast the Sega Mouse moves for the same hand movement."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:271
msgid "Hide Mouse Pointer:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:271
msgid "Hide the pointer over the game after it has not moved for this long (in a window and in fullscreen). Moving the mouse shows it again."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:271
msgid "Always: hides it over the game all the time."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:271
msgid "In Sega Pico games the pointer is always hidden whatever is chosen here (the game draws the pen itself)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:272
msgid "Invert Mouse Y Axis"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:272
msgid "Flip the up/down movement of the Sega Mouse."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:273
msgid "Confine Mouse to the Picture"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:273
msgid "While a Sega Mouse or light gun game runs, keep the pointer inside the picture. Pause the game, press the Windows key or press Alt+Tab to let it go; a click in the picture holds it again."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:276
msgid "Light Gun"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:277
msgid "Gun Aim Offset X:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:277
msgid "Shifts where the light gun hits, sideways, if shots land beside where you point."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:278
msgid "Gun Aim Offset Y:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:278
msgid "Shifts where the light gun hits, up or down, if shots land above or below where you point."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:279
msgid "Show Light Gun Crosshair"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:279
msgid "Show a crosshair instead of the arrow over the picture while a light gun is plugged in."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:280
msgid "Crosshair Shape:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:280
msgid "The look of the light gun crosshair: the cross Windows has, or one of eight shapes drawn by the program: plus, plus with a gap, dot, circle with a dot, circle with ticks, crosshair target, diamond and diamond with a dot."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:281
msgid "Crosshair Color:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:281
msgid "The color of the crosshair (it has an outline of the opposite brightness, so it shows on a bright and a dark picture). Not for the Windows Cross."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:282
msgid "Crosshair Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:282
msgid "How big the crosshair is. Not for the Windows Cross."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:283
msgid "Light Gun Border:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:283
msgid "While a light gun is plugged in, draws a white frame round the picture (the picture is made smaller to leave room for it). Guns that see the screen with a camera, such as the Sinden Lightgun, need it to find the picture. The thickness is a share of the window's height."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:290
msgid "Region:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:290
msgid "Which region the console reports to the game: detect from the ROM, USA, Europe or Japan."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:291
msgid "Force VDP Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:291
msgid "Run the video timing at 60 Hz (NTSC) or 50 Hz (PAL) whatever the region says."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:292
msgid "Console:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:292
msgid "Detect the console from the game, or force a specific model."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:293
msgid "Lock-On Cartridge:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:293
msgid "Attach a Game Genie, Action Replay or Sonic & Knuckles to the game."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:294
msgid "Boot from BIOS When Available"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:294
msgid "Run the console's start-up ROM when a BIOS file is present in the BIOS folder."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:295
msgid "Show Extended Game Gear Screen"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:295
msgid "Show the whole Game Gear picture instead of only the part its screen displayed."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:296
msgid "Show Master System Side Borders"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:296
msgid "Show the 8-pixel columns at each side of the picture, which are otherwise cropped."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:298
msgid "Accuracy"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:299
msgid "Emulate Address Error Exceptions"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:299
msgid "Hardware-accurate crashes on bad memory access; turn off only for a few hacked games."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:300
msgid "Enhanced Per-Tile Vertical Scroll"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:300
msgid "Lets each 8-pixel tile column scroll up and down on its own, instead of in 16-pixel pairs, by averaging with its neighbour: smooths the jagged edges some games make in 2-cell vertical scroll mode. It changes only the few games that use that mode, and can show glitches."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:301
msgid "Vertical Scroll Limit:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:301
msgid "Only with Enhanced Per-Tile Vertical Scroll: where the difference in scroll between two neighbouring tiles is this many pixels or more, the enhancement is not used there."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:302
msgid "System Lock-Ups"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:302
msgid "Emulate the lock-ups the real console has when a game reads or writes an address that does not exist. Turn off only for a few demos and homebrew that rely on such accesses to work."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:303
msgid "CPU Speed:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:303
msgid "Speed of the emulated main 68000 (and the Mega CD's second 68000): above 100% can reduce slowdown in games that slow down, but can cause glitches or break timing; below 100% slows the game's logic down. The sound chips are not changed. Always 100% during netplay. Applies at once."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:304
msgid "Emulate Mega CD Disc Latency"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:304
msgid "Make the Mega CD drive seek slowly like the real one; some games need it; off makes loading instant."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:306
msgid "Video Recording"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:307
msgid "Recording Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:307
msgid "How big a video recording (Video + Audio) is, as a multiple of the console's own picture: 1x is for example 320x224, 2x (default) 640x448, 4x 1280x896. Enlarged in whole pixels, so it stays sharp. Used for the next recording."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:308
msgid "Recording Quality:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:308
msgid "How much data a second of video gets. Automatic (default) picks it from the size. A higher value keeps more detail and makes a bigger file. Used for the next recording."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:309
msgid "Recording Shape:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:309
msgid "The shape of the recorded video. Used for the next recording."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:309
msgid "Console's Own: keeps the picture's pixels square."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:309
msgid "4:3 (TV): stretches it to the shape of a TV, as the game looks in the window."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:309
msgid "16:9 (Widescreen): stretches it over a widescreen video."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "Screenshot Output:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "What a screenshot contains."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "Corrected (default): the picture stretched to the 4:3 shape of a TV, 640x480."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "Raw: the console's own picture at its own size (for example 320x224), not stretched."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "Final: like Corrected, but with the Render Filter applied (the same as Corrected when no filter is on)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:312
msgid "Scanlines and Brighten are never included."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:313
msgid "Screenshot Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:313
msgid "How big a screenshot is, as a multiple of the size the chosen output has."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:313
msgid "1x: 640x480 for Corrected, the console's own size for Raw and the filter's size for Final."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:313
msgid "2x, 3x, 4x: that many times larger (Raw is enlarged in whole pixels, so it stays sharp)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:316
msgid "Rewind and Resume"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:317
msgid "Enable Rewind"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:317
msgid "Keep a history of recent play so holding Backspace runs it backwards. Uses 256 MB by default (Rewind History Size) and some CPU; not available during netplay."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:318
msgid "Rewind Speed:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:318
msgid "How fast Rewind runs backwards. Faster rewinds go through the history sooner."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:318
msgid "Slow: half the speed of Normal (the default)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:318
msgid "Fast: twice as fast."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:318
msgid "Very Fast: four times as fast."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:319
msgid "Rewind History Size:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:319
msgid "How much memory the Rewind history may use: the more, the further back you can go (how far depends on the game). Changing it starts the history again. Large sizes may not be available in the 32-bit program."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:320
msgid "Auto Resume"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:320
msgid "Save the game when you close it, and offer to carry on from there the next time you open it."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:322
msgid "Start and Backup"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:323
msgid "Backup State:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:323
msgid "While a game runs, quietly save its state every few minutes into a rolling backup (Emulation > Load State > Load Backup State), so a crash never loses much."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:324
msgid "Skip Intro on Launch:"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:324
msgid "When a game starts, run its first few seconds of game time at full speed to get past logos and intros; this takes much less real time than the seconds chosen. A press of a game button ends the skip early. Not used when resuming a game or during netplay."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:326
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:327
msgid "Enable Log"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:327
msgid "Write genesis-plus-ex.log in the Logs folder next to the program (Settings > General > Data Folders can move it): the machine and the whole settings file at the start; for each game its file, ROM header, console set-up, picture and sound and, at the end, how it went (time, frames, slowest frame); resets, states, cheat codes and their timing, messages, errors and netplay events. Send it along with a bug report."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:328
msgid "Enable Crash Report"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:328
msgid "If the program crashes, write genesis-plus-ex-crash.txt and genesis-plus-ex-crash.dmp in the Logs folder next to the program (only the last crash is kept). Send both with a bug report."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:329
msgid "Factory Reset..."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:329
msgid "Delete the settings file (both copies, the one beside the program and the one in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Genesis Plus EX, when each exists) and start the program again as a new copy would: every setting, the controls, the hotkeys, the picture adjustments, the audio levels, the folders and ROM directories, the favorites and the lists of recent and played games go back to their defaults. Your games, saves, save states, covers, cheat files and saved controller profiles are not touched. Restore Defaults (below) only puts back the settings of this window."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:336
msgid "General"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:340
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:988
msgid "Each direction is compared with the deadzone on its own, so a small push off a straight line already adds a second direction (up becomes up and left). The way the program has always worked."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:989
msgid "Up, down, left and right get wide zones and the diagonals narrow ones: a stick that drifts a little still runs straight (Sonic does not roll or crouch by accident)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:990
msgid "All eight directions get the same share of the circle: a balanced choice for games that use diagonals as much as straight lines."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:991
msgid "The diagonals get wide zones and the straight directions narrow ones: up-right, down-left and so on are easy to hit, for games that need diagonals."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1055
msgid "Chaos Emerald"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1407
msgid "Restore Dialog"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1410
msgid "Bring back the \"Resume where you left off?\" question if you ticked \"Do not ask again\". Grayed while the question is already switched on."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1441
msgid "Enable Shader"
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1458
msgid "Clear the shader path (this turns the shader off)."
msgstr ""

#: settings.c:1459
